        "created_at": "2018-06-28T12:09:33.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"MindWanderer\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/mindwanderer/48/4816_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> MindWanderer:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<div><b>dprcooke wrote:</b><br><blockquote>That's certainly an interesting analysis, Mindwanderer.&nbsp; I could definitely see why some groups would want to drop the initiative system (either for more in-character time* or faster play).&nbsp; The biggest problem I've had running the starter kit is players taking \"too long\" to dedide what they want to do on their turn, and that has been solved by having players say who they'll pass to at the start of their own turn, so that the next person can start thinking about what they want to do earlier.&nbsp; If I were to drop the system, I'd probably adopt the order set by the card game for simplicity.\n&nbsp;\n</blockquote></div>\nHaving a strict turn sequence system would achieve the same thing, and then some, since you'll know when your turn is coming up a whole round in advance.\n<div><b>dprcooke wrote:</b><br><blockquote>*Interestingly, the starter kit provides an <em>alternative</em> way to think about a game of the RPG: it suggests that the players/GM are a group of comic-book writers putting together a story, with the GM acting as editor-in-chief.&nbsp; Now, pretending to be a guy writing about Absolute Zero seems to me FAR more boring than pretending to be Absolute Zero, so I tossed this suggestion aside in less time than it took to read it.&nbsp; But if you&nbsp;<em>were</em> to play the game imagining that you're the writing staff, the current initiative system is perfectly in-character.&nbsp; \"Oh! It would be so cool if Abs. Zero were to go next, he could do [this]!\"\n&nbsp;\n</blockquote></div>\nYeah, I don't think that makes a ton of sense.&nbsp; Each player represents a hero, and their motivation isn't to tell a good story, it's to overcome the challenges the GM throws at them.&nbsp; There are ways you could structure a game where everyone represents a writer in editiorial control of their favorite character, but this isn't it.\n<div><b>dprcooke wrote:</b><br><blockquote>As to the episode itself, I didn't manage to listen to the whole thing (I started the episode too close to bedtime to watch the whole thing), but the second that Christopher put overcome tickboxes on the relic-things it was obvious to me that the goal of the scene was to deal with them and not the dragon.&nbsp; I'm surprised to read that they didn't until closer to the end of scene.&nbsp; (That said, just because it was obvious to me doesn't mean it should be to everyone).\n&nbsp;\n</blockquote></div>\nHaving read through the starter kit, I wasn't sure of this at all.&nbsp; There are a lot of \"checkbox\" elements that are entirely optional, and many aren't even good uses of your time.\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I think we'll have to disagree there. The goal of an ROG isn't just to overcome the GM's challenges, but also to work together with the GM to tell a great story.&nbsp;</p>",
